# provision.py builds the suite venv at <suite_root>/.venv - i.e. inside this repo -
# so every installed copy is dirty without this, and one `git add -A` commits a few
# hundred MB of platform-specific virtualenv.
.venv/
.ruff_cache/

# The workflow harness creates its per-agent worktrees under .claude/worktrees/,
# and each one carries a `.git` FILE rather than a directory. `git add -A` in the
# repo root therefore records them as GITLINKS - bogus submodule entries no clone
# can resolve - which is a worse version of the drift-swept-into-a-commit problem,
# and one that a published copy would hand to every clone.
#
# Scoped to worktrees/ rather than all of .claude/ ON PURPOSE (gh#51): a future
# checked-in .claude/settings.json or project skill stays trackable. Ignoring the
# whole directory would be easier and would quietly take that option away.
.claude/worktrees/

# Backups (`agentflow.backups`). The canonical name and every legacy family put text AFTER
# the `.bak` marker, so `*.bak` alone would miss most of them - hence a pattern per shape
# rather than the single `*.bak*` that would also swallow an innocent `recipe.baked.md`.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING IT DOES MORE THAN IT DOES: `data_root` lives OUTSIDE this repo,
# so this rule is not what bounds the backup tree - `python -m agentflow.backups reap` is.
# What this prevents is narrower and still worth having: a stray backup taken while editing
# the suite tree itself, committed because `git add -A` could not tell it apart from source.
*.bak
*.bak.*
*.bak-*
*.bak[0-9]*
